1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,710
You know that feeling when
you learn something new...

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:05,810
and suddenly, it seems incredibly simple?

3
00:00:06,585 --> 00:00:07,685
You watch a couple of videos.

4
00:00:07,765 --> 00:00:09,575
Read a few articles.

5
00:00:09,965 --> 00:00:11,555
Maybe you try it yourself.

6
00:00:12,549 --> 00:00:14,379
And then you think: "I get it."

7
00:00:15,205 --> 00:00:16,875
But here's the scary part.

8
00:00:17,797 --> 00:00:20,437
What if the thing you're
missing is precisely the thing

9
00:00:20,517 --> 00:00:22,647
that would tell you that
you're missing something?

10
00:00:23,419 --> 00:00:26,849
This is the basic idea behind
the Dunning–Kruger effect.

11
00:00:27,803 --> 00:00:30,103
And it's much more
interesting — and much more

12
00:00:30,183 --> 00:00:33,093
disturbing — than the popular
internet version of it.

13
00:00:35,483 --> 00:00:37,713
Imagine you've never programmed before.

14
00:00:38,403 --> 00:00:40,553
Someone shows you a few lines of code.

15
00:00:41,362 --> 00:00:43,232
You learn what a variable is.

16
00:00:43,702 --> 00:00:45,172
You learn what a loop does.

17
00:00:45,983 --> 00:00:47,893
You write your first little program...

18
00:00:48,123 --> 00:00:51,673
and suddenly programming doesn't
seem that difficult anymore.

19
00:00:52,573 --> 00:00:54,853
You might even look at
professional programmers and

20
00:00:54,933 --> 00:00:58,263
wonder: "Why do they make
such a big deal out of this?"

21
00:00:59,069 --> 00:01:00,269
But then you keep learning.

22
00:01:01,469 --> 00:01:03,279
You encounter data structures.

23
00:01:03,409 --> 00:01:05,959
Concurrency. Memory management.

24
00:01:06,964 --> 00:01:10,274
And suddenly you realise that
programming isn't one problem.

25
00:01:11,014 --> 00:01:14,754
It's an entire landscape of problems
you didn't even know existed.

26
00:01:15,617 --> 00:01:16,817
And that's the key.

27
00:01:19,107 --> 00:01:22,747
In nineteen ninety-nine,
psychologists David Dunning and Justin

28
00:01:22,827 --> 00:01:26,857
Kruger published a study
investigating something very similar.

29
00:01:27,608 --> 00:01:31,458
They were interested in people's
ability to judge their own performance.

30
00:01:32,568 --> 00:01:36,418
They gave students tests of logical
reasoning, grammar, and humour.

31
00:01:37,288 --> 00:01:39,898
Then they asked them how well
they thought they had done.

32
00:01:40,645 --> 00:01:44,975
What they reported was that people
who performed poorly on certain tasks

33
00:01:45,205 --> 00:01:49,035
often had difficulty recognising
just how poorly they had performed.

34
00:01:50,042 --> 00:01:51,652
And the numbers were striking.

35
00:01:52,634 --> 00:01:55,674
The people who performed in the
bottom quarter estimated their

36
00:01:55,754 --> 00:01:59,644
performance as if they were around
the sixty-second percentile.

37
00:02:00,517 --> 00:02:02,507
In reality, they were around the twelfth.

38
00:02:03,663 --> 00:02:07,433
They weren't just wrong. They were
wrong about how wrong they were.

39
00:02:09,493 --> 00:02:12,863
In other words, the problem wasn't
only that they made mistakes.

40
00:02:13,913 --> 00:02:17,843
They also lacked some of the knowledge
necessary to recognise those mistakes.

41
00:02:18,786 --> 00:02:21,056
And this creates a peculiar situation.

42
00:02:22,167 --> 00:02:25,077
You need a certain amount of
knowledge to perform a task.

43
00:02:25,987 --> 00:02:27,607
But you may also need some of that same

44
00:02:27,687 --> 00:02:30,097
knowledge to accurately
judge your performance.

45
00:02:31,076 --> 00:02:33,086
So when your knowledge is very limited...

46
00:02:33,396 --> 00:02:37,346
your ability to evaluate your
own knowledge can be limited too.

47
00:02:38,093 --> 00:02:41,263
And this is where the phenomenon
becomes genuinely interesting.

48
00:02:42,221 --> 00:02:46,671
Suppose I give you a complicated
problem. You produce an answer.

49
00:02:47,321 --> 00:02:49,431
You are completely convinced it's correct.

50
00:02:50,342 --> 00:02:52,252
How can you tell whether you're right?

51
00:02:53,147 --> 00:02:55,117
You could compare it with an expert.

52
00:02:55,827 --> 00:02:57,537
You could test it against reality.

53
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:02,570
You could look for counterexamples.
You could measure the result.

54
00:03:03,455 --> 00:03:06,005
But imagine that none of
those things are available.

55
00:03:06,915 --> 00:03:09,345
Then you're relying largely
on your own judgement.

56
00:03:10,273 --> 00:03:12,593
And if your judgement is
based on the same incomplete

57
00:03:12,673 --> 00:03:15,343
knowledge that produced
the original answer...

58
00:03:15,553 --> 00:03:17,143
you have a problem.

59
00:03:17,951 --> 00:03:20,641
Your brain is essentially
grading its own exam.

60
00:03:21,551 --> 00:03:23,401
With an answer key it doesn't have.

61
00:03:26,341 --> 00:03:30,431
This is also why expertise can look
surprisingly different from confidence.

62
00:03:31,365 --> 00:03:33,695
A beginner might say: "This is easy."

63
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:37,570
An experienced person
might say: "It depends."

64
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:42,070
Why? Not necessarily because
the expert is less confident.

65
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:45,270
The expert simply sees more variables.

66
00:03:46,235 --> 00:03:47,885
The beginner sees the problem.

67
00:03:48,805 --> 00:03:50,485
The expert sees the problem...

68
00:03:50,565 --> 00:03:53,275
and all the ways the problem can go wrong.

69
00:03:54,051 --> 00:03:56,841
Think about something as
simple as fixing a computer.

70
00:03:57,795 --> 00:04:01,585
A beginner might say: "Just
reinstall the operating system."

71
00:04:02,457 --> 00:04:04,647
Sometimes, that's exactly right.

72
00:04:05,518 --> 00:04:09,448
But an experienced administrator
immediately starts asking questions.

73
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:11,830
Is the hardware healthy?

74
00:04:11,910 --> 00:04:15,780
Is the filesystem corrupted?
Is the disk failing?

75
00:04:16,551 --> 00:04:19,361
Is there a backup? Is the
problem actually software?

76
00:04:20,211 --> 00:04:21,561
Is there a network issue?

77
00:04:22,452 --> 00:04:25,062
Will reinstalling destroy useful evidence?

78
00:04:25,912 --> 00:04:27,922
What caused the problem
in the first place?

79
00:04:28,758 --> 00:04:30,668
The beginner sees one solution.

80
00:04:31,378 --> 00:04:33,248
The expert sees a decision tree.

81
00:04:34,083 --> 00:04:37,253
And that difference can make
expertise look like uncertainty.

82
00:04:39,993 --> 00:04:41,203
But there's an important catch.

83
00:04:42,691 --> 00:04:45,311
The Dunning–Kruger effect
does not mean: "The less

84
00:04:45,391 --> 00:04:48,241
intelligent you are, the
more confident you become."

85
00:04:49,038 --> 00:04:51,808
And it doesn't mean that
experts are always insecure.

86
00:04:52,798 --> 00:04:54,568
That's a caricature of the research.

87
00:04:55,664 --> 00:04:57,114
But there's another problem.

88
00:04:58,128 --> 00:05:00,108
What if even the famous
Dunning–Kruger graph

89
00:05:00,188 --> 00:05:02,298
doesn't mean quite what we think it means?

90
00:05:03,152 --> 00:05:05,372
Several researchers have
argued that the characteristic

91
00:05:05,452 --> 00:05:09,342
pattern can appear partly
because of statistical effects.

92
00:05:10,288 --> 00:05:12,218
Things like regression to the mean.

93
00:05:12,508 --> 00:05:16,078
And the fact that almost everyone
thinks they're above average.

94
00:05:16,648 --> 00:05:19,288
And a correlation created
by the way the two

95
00:05:19,368 --> 00:05:21,618
measurements are plotted
against each other.

96
00:05:22,313 --> 00:05:24,203
And here's the surprising part.

97
00:05:25,097 --> 00:05:27,517
If you take simulated
data with no such effect

98
00:05:27,597 --> 00:05:30,387
built into it, and plot it the same way...

99
00:05:31,124 --> 00:05:34,054
you can still get a graph that
looks much like the original.

100
00:05:34,996 --> 00:05:38,586
In other words... the graph itself
needs to be interpreted carefully.

101
00:05:41,416 --> 00:05:43,866
And the debate isn't completely settled.

102
00:05:44,776 --> 00:05:49,306
There are responses to those criticisms,
and responses to those responses.

103
00:05:50,376 --> 00:05:52,926
Which brings us back to
the uncomfortable question.

104
00:05:53,656 --> 00:05:55,126
How do you know you're right?

105
00:05:57,926 --> 00:06:00,636
Dunning and Kruger's own
claim was more subtle.

106
00:06:01,586 --> 00:06:05,396
People can have difficulty accurately
evaluating their own competence,

107
00:06:05,946 --> 00:06:09,716
especially when they lack the skills
needed to evaluate the task itself.

108
00:06:10,611 --> 00:06:12,741
And that can happen to almost anyone.

109
00:06:13,651 --> 00:06:16,561
In fact, that's what makes
this effect so dangerous.

110
00:06:17,437 --> 00:06:19,007
You don't have to be stupid.

111
00:06:19,577 --> 00:06:21,507
You just have to enter a new field.

112
00:06:22,378 --> 00:06:24,428
Every expert was once a beginner.

113
00:06:24,898 --> 00:06:29,508
Every beginner initially has a very
incomplete mental model of the subject.

114
00:06:30,433 --> 00:06:34,193
And that means every one of us has areas
where we may be confidently wrong...

115
00:06:34,273 --> 00:06:36,423
without realising it.

116
00:06:37,109 --> 00:06:39,599
And here's the really
uncomfortable question.

117
00:06:40,490 --> 00:06:42,680
How do you know you're
not doing it right now?

118
00:06:46,020 --> 00:06:48,980
You might be thinking:
"Fortunately, I understand the

119
00:06:49,060 --> 00:06:51,910
Dunning–Kruger effect, so
I'm not one of those people."

120
00:06:52,644 --> 00:06:54,314
But that doesn't prove anything.

121
00:06:55,104 --> 00:06:58,874
Knowing that the effect exists doesn't
magically make you immune to it.

122
00:06:59,569 --> 00:07:01,469
You can understand the
concept perfectly...

123
00:07:01,549 --> 00:07:04,999
and still overestimate your
knowledge of economics.

124
00:07:06,116 --> 00:07:08,426
Or medicine. Or programming. Or history.

125
00:07:09,412 --> 00:07:10,592
Or physics. Or politics.

126
00:07:10,672 --> 00:07:12,582
Or literally anything else.

127
00:07:14,872 --> 00:07:17,082
So what can we actually do about it?

128
00:07:17,869 --> 00:07:20,439
The answer isn't simply: "Be more humble."

129
00:07:21,357 --> 00:07:22,877
Humility is useful.

130
00:07:22,957 --> 00:07:26,667
But humility by itself doesn't
tell you whether you're right.

131
00:07:27,493 --> 00:07:29,073
A person can be extremely humble...

132
00:07:29,153 --> 00:07:31,083
and still be wrong.

133
00:07:31,864 --> 00:07:35,034
What we need is something
better: feedback from reality.

134
00:07:36,141 --> 00:07:40,391
Test your assumptions. Look for
counterexamples. Measure things.

135
00:07:41,253 --> 00:07:43,263
Ask people who know more than you.

136
00:07:43,853 --> 00:07:46,403
Try to predict what will
happen before it happens.

137
00:07:47,303 --> 00:07:49,033
And, perhaps most importantly...

138
00:07:49,163 --> 00:07:52,793
make it possible for yourself
to discover that you're wrong.

139
00:07:54,873 --> 00:07:59,023
A good programmer doesn't trust their
code because they feel confident about it.

140
00:07:59,553 --> 00:08:00,783
They run tests.

141
00:08:01,733 --> 00:08:05,323
A scientist doesn't trust an
idea because it feels convincing.

142
00:08:06,153 --> 00:08:07,843
They test it against evidence.

143
00:08:08,786 --> 00:08:12,636
An engineer doesn't say: "I'm
pretty sure this bridge will hold."

144
00:08:13,511 --> 00:08:16,631
They calculate it. Measure it. Test it.

145
00:08:16,711 --> 00:08:18,601
And build in safety margins.

146
00:08:19,542 --> 00:08:21,722
These systems work because they don't rely

147
00:08:21,802 --> 00:08:24,872
entirely on the human
ability to judge itself.

148
00:08:25,697 --> 00:08:27,867
They let reality provide the answer.

149
00:08:30,307 --> 00:08:33,997
And perhaps that's the most useful
lesson of the Dunning–Kruger effect.

150
00:08:34,861 --> 00:08:37,131
The goal isn't to
constantly doubt yourself.

151
00:08:37,961 --> 00:08:40,611
It's not: "Maybe I'm
wrong about everything."

152
00:08:41,487 --> 00:08:44,337
The goal is to know how you
would find out if you were wrong.

153
00:08:45,061 --> 00:08:47,951
That's a much stronger form
of intellectual humility.

154
00:08:48,933 --> 00:08:51,343
Instead of saying: "I'm probably right."

155
00:08:52,229 --> 00:08:54,799
Ask: "What evidence would
convince me I'm wrong?"

156
00:08:57,139 --> 00:08:58,629
If you have a good answer...

157
00:08:59,139 --> 00:09:01,589
you're already doing
something very valuable.

158
00:09:02,470 --> 00:09:06,260
Because the most dangerous ignorance
isn't simply not knowing something.

159
00:09:06,750 --> 00:09:08,940
It's not knowing that you don't know it.

160
00:09:11,710 --> 00:09:15,680
And sometimes... the first step
towards becoming an expert...

161
00:09:16,010 --> 00:09:19,220
is discovering just how
much there is left to learn.
